  "assistant_text": "I’ve set up a comparison model with three paths: keep Pro at £49, raise it to £59 with the release, or phase the increase. It cannot yet determine the implication because key inputs were not supplied.\n\nHere is one **single set of starting assumptions** to adopt or correct — these are **not measurements**:\n\n**Current starting point**\n- Monthly churn: **3%**, cautiously below the 4% constraint.\n- Pro subscriber base: **250 subscribers** — a round planning baseline, implying about **£12,250/month** at £49 before other plans.\n- Pro conversion rate: **5%** — neutral placeholder pending funnel data.\n- Perceived Pro value: **60%** — moderate, because no evidence was provided on the feature’s willingness-to-pay effect.\n- Price–release alignment: **50%** — neutral because the strength of the feature/price connection is unknown.\n- MRR per Pro subscriber: **£49/month**, matching the current price.\n\n**Option levels**\n- **Raise Pro to £59 at release:** price–release alignment **100%**, since the increase occurs with the release.\n- **Phase Pro price increase:** Pro price **£54/month** and price–release alignment **50%**. This is a midpoint assumption only; no actual phasing path was specified.\n- The existing model already has **£49/month** for keeping price flat and **£59/month** for the direct increase.\n\nIf you approve, I’ll save this whole starting point and run the comparison. The result will show what the model implies, and especially whether the £59 move risks the **under-4% churn** constraint.\n\nThe model was saved. Questions this model does not answer yet: Does the £59 price apply to existing Pro subscribers, new subscribers, or both? What is the current Pro subscriber base, conversion rate, and monthly churn rate? What feature is being released, and is its expected effect on perceived Pro value or retention known? Would a phased increase have a defined price path and timing before the 12-month horizon?",
